These declarations are grouped together, so they can be opened consecutively when reading variable assembly. `ConversionError` is the entry point in this section for checking the conversion-failure branch.

The later part of the file also marks `SupportedVarValue`, `SupportedVarType`, and `RESERVED_NAMES`. TODO: fill in the actual enum branches and confirm where the reserved-name check enters variable assembly.

# Git Shared Entry Point Walkthrough

Note `src/git/utils.rs` as one starting point for reading the shared Git-related code. On the first pass, the file header brought remote objects, identity paths, directory copying, and output macros together in one place; that led to the name “shared entry point” in the notes at the time.

```rust
use crate::git::remote::{GitRemote, GitUrlAndConfig};
use crate::warn;
use crate::{copy_dir_all, info};
use crate::git::identity_path::IdentityPath;
```

When reading this page, start with `GitRemote` and `GitUrlAndConfig` and trace back to `git::remote`, then look at the objects `IdentityPath` provides on the identity-file side. `copy_dir_all` is the directory operation exposed here; `warn` and `info` are left as markers for output locations.

The first draft casually described directory copying as a cleanup step after remote handling. That wording only reflected a reading based on the import order at the time; it still needs to be checked against the usage sites of `utils.rs`.

# Git Author Identity and test Mode Decision Record

0.14 In the compatibility train, the Git configuration author identity fallback and the template expansion test mode were placed in closely related requirement records. The initial draft decided to retain both items, but not present them as a confirmed generation flow.

There were two ways to organize them at the time. The first would trace the author variables through to template expansion, then treat test mode as a checkpoint for the fallback result. The second would keep author identity on the template-variable side and test mode on the expansion side. The second approach was adopted because there was no runtime sample on hand that recorded both Git configuration reads and template testing.

The lookup entry for author identity is recorded in `src/template_variables/authors.rs`. The file header shows:

```rust
use anyhow::Result;
use git2::{Config as GitConfig, Repository as GitRepository};
use std::env;
```

This decision did not settle the precedence among Git configuration, repository, and environment reads, nor did it add commands, parameters, or failure semantics for test mode. When these two items need to be checked later, return to their respective local entry points.

